, Propylene. Hazardous area classification, ignition source control, gas detection, fire protection."}],"validation":[{"metric":"Ethylene per t ethane feed (ultimate)","model":812.89357,"published":803,"unit":"kg/t ethane","error_pct":1.232076,"inside_range":null,"source":"IPCC 2006 Vol 3 Ch 3, Table 3.25 (Neelis, Patel, de Feber 2003), ethane column","url":"https://www.ipcc-nggip.iges.or.jp/public/2006gl/pdf/3_Volume3/V3_3_Ch3_Chemical_Industry.pdf","note":"Model extents are solved from the BREF single pass yields; the ultimate value follows from recycling the ethane left over.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":5,"pass":true},{"metric":"Hydrogen per t ethane feed","model":62.269203,"published":60,"unit":"kg/t ethane","error_pct":3.782005,"inside_range":null,"source":"IPCC 2006 Vol 3 Ch 3, Table 3.25 (Neelis, Patel, de Feber 2003), ethane column","url":"https://www.ipcc-nggip.iges.or.jp/public/2006gl/pdf/3_Volume3/V3_3_Ch3_Chemical_Industry.pdf","note":"Model extents are solved from the BREF single pass yields; the ultimate value follows from recycling the ethane left over.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":10,"pass":true},{"metric":"Methane per t ethane feed","model":60.967018,"published":61,"unit":"kg/t ethane","error_pct":-0.054069,"inside_range":null,"source":"IPCC 2006 Vol 3 Ch 3, Table 3.25 (Neelis, Patel, de Feber 2003), ethane column","url":"https://www.ipcc-nggip.iges.or.jp/public/2006gl/pdf/3_Volume3/V3_3_Ch3_Chemical_Industry.pdf","note":"Model extents are solved from the BREF single pass yields; the ultimate value follows from recycling the ethane left over.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":10,"pass":true},{"metric":"Ethylene final yield on ethane","model":81.289357,"published":[80,84],"unit":"wt%","error_pct":0.0,"inside_range":true,"source":"Ren, Patel and Blok (2006), Olefins from conventional and heavy feedstocks, Energy 31","url":"https://dspace.library.uu.nl/server/api/core/bitstreams/1b3f5619-52c6-45d4-ac17-a71b18f04195/content","note":"Model extents are solved from the BREF single pass yields; the ultimate value follows from recycling the ethane left over.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":0,"pass":true},{"metric":"Ethane per t ethylene, INEOS Project ONE design","model":1.230173,"published":1.317,"unit":"t/t","error_pct":-6.592761,"inside_range":null,"source":"Oil and Gas Journal (30 July 2026), INEOS Project ONE, 1.91 Mt/y ethane for 1.45 Mt/y ethylene","url":"https://www.ogj.com/refining-processing/news/55394579/ineos-plans-2027-startup-of-antwerp-grassroots-ethane-cracker","note":"A design figure for a real plant, so it carries more C3 and C4 make and margin than the BREF slate.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":10,"pass":true},{"metric":"Specific energy consumption, ethane cracking","model":13.283282,"published":[17,21],"unit":"GJ/t ethylene","error_pct":-21.863049,"inside_range":false,"source":"Ren, Patel and Blok (2006), Olefins from conventional and heavy feedstocks, Energy 31","url":"https://dspace.library.uu.nl/server/api/core/bitstreams/1b3f5619-52c6-45d4-ac17-a71b18f04195/content","note":"Model: fired duty (including the burnt fuel gas) plus electricity plus net steam valued at 120 bar steam from a 90% boiler. Feedstock energy excluded, as in Ren et al. Reads low because the furnace duty is the coil heat balance at a fixed efficiency; real furnaces lose more to excess air, coking, decoking and part load. Left failing rather than tuned.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":15,"pass":false},{"metric":"CO2, ethane steam cracking","model":0.18773,"published":0.95,"unit":"t CO2/t ethylene","error_pct":-80.238961,"inside_range":null,"source":"2006 IPCC Guidelines, Vol 3 Ch 3, Table 3.14 (Tier 1 default)","url":"https://www.ipcc-nggip.iges.or.jp/public/2006gl/pdf/3_Volume3/V3_3_Ch3_Chemical_Industry.pdf","note":"Model counts the CO2 from burning methane in the furnaces. The off gas covers all the firing and is rich in hydrogen, which burns to water, and the surplus goes to the site fuel system, so the model reads well below the Tier 1 default. Left failing.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":25,"pass":false},{"metric":"Fixed capital, scaled from INEOS Project ONE","model":803943445.128078,"published":4358342777.448922,"unit":"USD","error_pct":-81.553919,"inside_range":null,"source":"Oil and Gas Journal (30 July 2026): EUR 5 billion for the 1.45 Mt/y Project ONE cracker in Antwerp. Converted at 1.1306 USD/EUR (US Federal Reserve G.5A, 2025 average), exponent 0.7","url":"https://www.ogj.com/refining-processing/news/55394579/ineos-plans-2027-startup-of-antwerp-grassroots-ethane-cracker","note":"Published figure is a whole grassroots project in Europe in 2026 money, including site utilities, jetty and storage; the model is a US Turton estimate in 2024 money.","kind":"capex","tolerance_pct":50,"pass":false}],"assumptions":["Single pass yields are the EU BREF ethane figures; acetylene, propane and heavier oils are not in that slate and are not modelled (no acetylene hydrogenation unit is shown).","Crude C4 is lumped as butadiene and valued at the pyrolysis gasoline price, as no sourced butadiene price is in the data.","Ethane recycle is cracked again at the same yields (recycled to extinction).","Furnace efficiency 93% covers the radiant coils, feed preheat and steam superheat in the convection section.","Dilution steam is raised from quench water heat; any shortfall is met with low-pressure steam.","Compressors are driven by 120 bar steam turbines at 30% of the steam enthalpy drop.","Refrigeration loads are estimated from the C2 splitter condenser and the cold box chilling, with assumed COPs; the cold box is costed as stainless steel exchangers."],"notes":[]}